<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What causes cheek swelling after a root canal?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Mild cheek swelling for 24–48 hours after a root canal is normal as the body responds to the procedure. However, worsening swelling, fever, or increasing pain after 3 days suggests a failed root canal or persistent infection requiring re-treatment or extraction. Contact CARE Hospitals immediately if swelling worsens after dental treatment.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can a swollen cheek be serious?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — cheek swelling from a dental infection can spread into facial spaces (buccal, submasseteric, submandibular), potentially compromising the airway. If the swelling is rapidly worsening, causing difficulty opening your mouth, swallowing, or breathing, seek emergency care immediately at CARE Hospitals.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How long does cheek swelling take to resolve?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">With appropriate treatment of the underlying cause, most cheek swelling resolves within 5–14 days. Antibiotics alone without source control may reduce swelling temporarily, but the swelling returns once antibiotics are stopped. Definitive treatment (root canal, extraction, or drainage) is required for complete resolution.</div>
</details>
